WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:07.049
Imagine what would happen if, millions of
years into the future, scientists — perhaps

00:00:07.049 --> 00:00:12.330
from other planets — tried to create depictions
of the long extinct organisms they found in

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:17.710
Earth’s fossil record. Perhaps they’d
create images of the deadly swans, who spear

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:23.220
prey with their long, hooked forearms. Or
of the predatory hippo — a behemoth with

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:28.990
imposing fangs and deadly jaws. But wait,
that’s not what a hippo looks like, or what

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:34.430
a swan looks like. These future scientists
are way off! Well, these are examples of how,

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:39.700
when recreating extinct species in those extreme
cases where one lacks any information on muscle

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:45.030
and fatty tissue and only has the skeleton
to work with, things can get a little off.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:50.110
These images are part of a thought experiment,
“All Todays,” written in part by the astonishing

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:54.800
author C.M. Koseman — whose work you might
have seen from his other most famous book

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.079
“All Tomorrows.” “All Todays” is a
section in the larger 2012 art book “All

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:05.519
Yesterdays,” dreamed up by not just C. M.
Kosemen, but Darren Naish — a renowned zoologist,

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:11.260
paleontologist and author, and John Conway:
an immensely talented paleo-artist. These

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:15.509
three are incredible creators, and I have
links to where you can follow and support

00:01:15.509 --> 00:01:19.840
them in this video’s description. And their
‘All Todays’ thought experiment of how

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.649
imaginary future scientists might grossly
misinterpret animals we’re familiar with

00:01:24.649 --> 00:01:29.499
is worth looking at further. The section is
intended to help people think critically about

00:01:29.499 --> 00:01:34.140
the ways in which. we’ve portrayed past
animals ourselves, like dinosaurs, in ways

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.539
that likely aren’t quite accurate. Early
paleo artists sometimes portrayed dinosaurs

00:01:38.539 --> 00:01:44.700
as emaciated creatures with exaggerated monstrous
features, a trend sometimes referred to as

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:49.229
‘shrink wrapping,’ which Kosemen, Naish,
and Conway have helped push against with all

00:01:49.229 --> 00:01:53.729
Yesterdays. Now, it’s worth noting, some
people who haven’t actually read the book

00:01:53.729 --> 00:01:58.249
have assumed these illustrations are mocking
the mistakes past paleontologists made in

00:01:58.249 --> 00:02:04.049
recreating dinosaurs, which isn’t the case.
“All Yesterdays” is imagining an entertaining

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:12.110
worst-case scenario where no muscle or skin
have been preserved on the fossils of familiar

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:21.500
modern animals. And the result is truly worth
documenting in the Archive. So, let’s get

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:26.620
started, and look at the ways in which paleontologists
of the future might imagine the species of

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:31.950
the present. The first image the hypothetical
future scientists have created of life from

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:38.430
Earth’s past is this illustration of a deadly
hunter, rendered in fearsome detail by John

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:43.850
Conway. But don’t worry too much, because
this is an erroneous interpretation of a house

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:48.730
cat. That’s right, the furry, whiskered
felines which populate our homes have, without

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:54.340
any reference for the future paleontologists
to go off of, been conceptualized as scaly,

00:02:54.340 --> 00:03:00.150
frightening beasts. A pretty amusing misreading,
although modern cats, despite their appearance,

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:05.710
are indeed proficient predators. Next, we
have a portrait of a bony, agile organism

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:10.481
— which honestly resembles some of the creatures
out of Kosemen’s All Tomorrows. Despite

00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:16.320
all appearances, however, this is a hypothetical
erroneous recreation of a Zebra. You’d be

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.970
forgiven if you didn’t make the connection,
as most of the features one associates with

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:26.120
the animals, like their stripes, mane, and
overall body shape, have been left out. The

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:31.430
future paleontologists have also misunderstood
the animal’s hooves, instead guessing the

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:35.930
organism has strange, single-toed feet on
which they run. The hooves which Zebras and

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:41.190
other equids use to get around on are pretty
unique in the animal kingdom, so it makes

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:47.590
sense why such an adaptation might not occur
to future scientists. This emaciated, angular,

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:53.120
reptilian looking beast is a hypothetical
misinterpretation of a Rhinoceros — which

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.890
misses the animal’s thick, protective skin
and overall heavyset body structure. The recreation

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:04.590
also sports a bizarre sail-like frill on the
animal’s back, which the imagined scientists

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:10.110
believed helped the animal shed excess heat.
This erroneous sail comes from a misunderstanding

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:14.951
surrounding the pronounced vertebrate on the
skeleton of the real-life animal. Another

00:04:14.951 --> 00:04:20.109
notable error? The fictional recreation is
also missing one of the most well-known features

00:04:20.109 --> 00:04:25.580
of the Rhino: its horn. As Rhino horns are
made of keratin, and wouldn’t fossilize

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:30.180
well like the other bones in the animal’s
skeleton — any future paleontologists might

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:35.100
be left in the dark about the animal’s most
distinguishing characteristic. Another animal

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:40.689
missing its most distinguishing characteristic
— and looking unrecognizable as a result

00:04:40.689 --> 00:04:45.860
— is the elephant, pictured here bereft
of its trunk. This is an intriguing example

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:51.061
of how an imagined reconstruction of a modern
animal might be too cautious. After all, the

00:04:51.061 --> 00:04:56.930
very concept of an elephant’s trunk is unique
and downright peculiar even in context, so

00:04:56.930 --> 00:05:02.060
one can imagine why it wouldn’t occur to
a future paleontologist. Especially when you

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:06.939
consider that the elephant’s trunk is a
muscular appendage without any internal skeletal

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:11.999
structure, meaning it wouldn’t be preserved
in any kind of fossil record. There are aspects

00:05:11.999 --> 00:05:16.409
of an elephant’s skull which do hint at
the existence of a trunk, but it’s not hard

00:05:16.409 --> 00:05:21.150
to imagine that a careful scientist of the
future might err on the side of caution and

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:27.469
dismiss such a possibility. The next erroneous
reconstruction shows a thick-furred herbivore

00:05:27.469 --> 00:05:33.270
in a mountainous habitat. What species is
this image based on? The manatee! An animal

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:38.570
which is certainly not furry and certainly
not terrestrial. But the authors imagine the

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:43.289
future paleontologists only have access to
the animal’s skull, which resembles that

00:05:43.289 --> 00:05:47.970
of a standard grass-dwelling herbivore. So
imagining the rest of the animal would be

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:53.059
a four legged, furry land-dweller isn’t
too big a leap — even if it’s obviously

00:05:53.059 --> 00:05:58.360
incorrect Another hypothetical interpretation
which misses the mark is this portrait of

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:03.819
a lithe, deadly predator that runs on all
fours to intercept prey. We know this animal

00:06:03.819 --> 00:06:09.159
as a baboon — and also know it’s quite
different from what the fictional paleontologists

00:06:09.159 --> 00:06:15.559
imagined it to be: possessing fur and an arboreal
lifestyle. The future scientists also believed

00:06:15.559 --> 00:06:21.389
the animal secreted deadly venom from its
front teeth. Baboons actually do possess unique

00:06:21.389 --> 00:06:26.099
fang grooves — a feature sometimes found
in venomous animals — for reasons that aren’t

00:06:26.099 --> 00:06:32.139
completely clear. So, while the concept of
a venomous baboon seems absurd to us, someone

00:06:32.139 --> 00:06:37.749
lacking all knowledge of the animal might
make that very mistake. Real paleontologists

00:06:37.749 --> 00:06:43.789
have made similar errors, like theories that
Cretaceous Period dinosaur Sinornithosaurus

00:06:43.789 --> 00:06:49.199
possessed a venomous bite due to its grooved
teeth — a hypothesis that was later proven

00:06:49.199 --> 00:06:54.509
false. Even more frightening than a venomous
baboon, we have this long-fingered predator

00:06:54.509 --> 00:06:59.650
interpreted as something right out of a nightmare.
You can really see Koseman’s All Tomorrows

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:04.809
style coming through here. But in our time,
we know the species as something much less

00:07:04.809 --> 00:07:10.479
frightening: the tiny, furry spider monkey.
Here, our imagined future paleontologists

00:07:10.479 --> 00:07:16.249
interpreted the uniquely long finger bones
present in spider monkey skeletons as gaunt,

00:07:16.249 --> 00:07:20.909
claw-like digits. And I think we can all be
glad that this is a species that doesn’t

00:07:20.909 --> 00:07:26.280
actually exist in this form. Moving on from
the primates, the next animal recreation from

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:31.460
our fictionalized scientists is a parasite
that sucks the blood from larger animals.

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:35.759
The image might be unnerving, but the real
animal it’s based on certainly isn’t.

00:07:35.759 --> 00:07:40.360
That’s right, it’s the one-and-only humming
bird — a fast moving, brightly colored bird

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:45.501
which gets its sustenance from nectar, instead
of from any kind of helpless animal. Hummingbirds

00:07:45.501 --> 00:07:50.490
are so innocent looking, it’s hard to imagine
anyone interpreting this animal as anything

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:55.919
frightening. But humming bird skeletons are
pretty weird, with strange proportions and

00:07:55.919 --> 00:08:00.610
long, serrated beaks. We know the curiously
lengthy beaks of humming birds are for the

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:05.539
purpose of accessing nectar, but this might
not be easily discernible without context.

00:08:05.539 --> 00:08:11.449
So, who’s to say imagined future paleontologists
might not land on a theory that hummingbirds

00:08:11.449 --> 00:08:17.360
are blood-sucking parasites? Here’s a second
misinterpretation of a species of bird, imagined

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:23.010
as another featherless, gaunt animal with
claws at the end of the wings. The unique

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:27.960
shape of their beaks signals that this animal
is a recreation of a Hornbill, a bird which

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:32.729
already looks like it’s from another time.
If you’re wondering why our future scientists

00:08:32.729 --> 00:08:37.450
assume both this and the hummingbird didn’t
have feathers, it’s likely because feathers

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:42.410
aren’t always preserved clearly in the fossil
record. And indeed, a similar thing happened

00:08:42.410 --> 00:08:47.560
when paleontologists originally missed that
dinosaurs like velociraptors had feathers.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:52.990
And so, the Hornbills are drawn as scaly beasts,
unable to take flight. Nearing the end of

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:57.640
our glimpse at “All Todays,” we have a
portrait of an animal with an usually long

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:03.960
body and four stumpy legs. Despite appearances,
this is a theoretical misunderstanding of

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:08.720
a snake. To us, it’s obvious that snakes
don’t have legs — I mean, just look at

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:13.490
them. But to someone who only knew a snake
through fragmentary remains, they might assume

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:18.570
the skeleton they were exhuming was incomplete.
After all, it does seem hard to believe that

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:23.810
the snake, unlike almost all other related
reptiles one might uncover, simply wouldn’t

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:29.560
have the main things animals used to get around.
And at the end of this limited preview of

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:34.630
“All Todays,” we have a speedy, bony organism
that looks like an animal you wouldn’t want

00:09:34.630 --> 00:09:40.590
to cross. So, what creature have the future
paleontologists misjudged this time? The cow.

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:46.790
Yep, the bulky grass-chewing herbivores themselves.
Cows are so ingrained in our modern consciousness,

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:52.150
the notion of anyone imagining these animals
as lithe, agile organisms certainly seems

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:57.500
silly. But, maybe you have to have seen a
cow to understand. And that’s the end of

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:02.750
our glimpse at the “All Todays” section
of Kosemen, Naish, and Conway’s unbelievably

00:10:02.750 --> 00:10:07.960
cool “All Yesterdays.” If you liked this
content, please consider supporting Kosemen,

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:12.090
Naish, and Conway’s incredible work by purchasing
a copy of “All Yesterdays” — and by

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:16.920
patronizing their other works and social media,
which again you can find in the links below.

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:22.320
Also, thanks for watching. If you enjoyed
this entry, please lend your support and like,

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:27.371
subscribe, and hit the notification icon to
stay up to date on all things Curious. See

00:10:27.371 --> 00:10:31.440
you in the next video.

